    "narrative": "Our primary offensive engine today is centered on high-upside matchups against favorable arms. **James Wood** (78.42) remains our undisputed alpha play, benefiting from a soft matchup against Eduardo Rodriguez and his elite sprint speed profile, which gains extra utility against a pitcher prone to allowing baserunners. **Tyler Soderstrom** (73.70) and **Brice Turang** (71.09) follow close behind; both benefit significantly from the matchup against Zebby Matthews and Tomoyuki Sugano, respectively. With **Brice Turang**\u2019s speed and **Tyler Soderstrom** holding a strong platoon advantage, these two are positioned to carry the weight in both the RBI and stolen base categories. **Corbin Carroll** (62.05) remains a staple; while Carson Palmquist is a tougher test, **Corbin Carroll**\u2019s speed-first profile ensures he remains an elite start regardless of the SP_xERA, as he is the primary engine for our scarcity-driven strategy.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision involved **Seiya Suzuki** (45.87), who sits on the bench today despite a solid 45.87 projection. Ultimately, the optimizer favored the platoon-neutral efficiency of **Max Muncy** and the defensive stability of **Gabriel Moreno**. **Seiya Suzuki**\u2019s matchup against Jared Jones\u2014a strikeout-heavy arm\u2014compressed his upside just enough to keep him out of the starting nine. Similarly, while **Vinnie Pasquantino** (42.72) is a high-floor asset, he was relegated to the bench due to a brutal matchup against Tarik Skubal, whose elite SP_xERA (2.66) creates too much drag for an efficient Roto output. \n\nInvestors should monitor the weather and final lineups closely, as many of our projections remain \"Lineup Pending.\" **Gabriel Moreno** and **Corbin Carroll** are facing Carson Palmquist, who is not a pushover; if the wind patterns at the stadium shift, their scoring potential may dip further below their current projections. We are also keeping a close eye on **Jose Caballero**; while his 50.82 score is bolstered by a strong platoon edge, his matchup against Jes\u00fas Luzardo carries high risk due to the opposing catcher's elite pop time, which effectively nullifies the stolen base value we crave. We proceed with the current configuration, banking on the high-speed profiles of **Cole Young** and **James Wood** to mitigate the volatility of today\u2019s matchups.",
